    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andriamasinavalona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andriamasinavalona.jpg" alt="Andriamasinavalona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andriamasinavalona">Andriamasinavalona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andriamasinavalona_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ny Maraina Randrianarisoa - Tanora taranak'Andriamasinavalona, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Merina, the people of the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>, descend from the island's Austronesian settlers, whose ancestors probably crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> during the first millennium. In the highlands small chiefdoms competed for the marshy valleys suited to irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Royal traditions, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantara-ny-andriana">Tantara ny Andriana</a></em> compiled in the nineteenth century, credit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamanelo">Andriamanelo</a>, conventionally dated to the mid-sixteenth century, with subduing the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vazimba">Vazimba</a> inhabitants with iron-tipped spears and founding the line of Merina kings, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralambo">Ralambo</a> with giving the kingdom its name, its royal talismans and its customs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> sacrifice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianjaka">Andrianjaka</a> is remembered as the king who first occupied the hill of Analamanga, renamed Antananarivo. Around 1700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamasinavalona">Andriamasinavalona</a> ruled an enlarged Imerina but divided it among his sons, beginning a century of rivalry between four kingdoms. Reunification came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a>, who seized power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambohimanga">Ambohimanga</a> around 1787, defeated his rivals and by about 1794 had made Antananarivo his capital. Declaring that the sea would be the border of his rice field, he left his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> a programme of conquest. Radama's alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1817 turned Imerina into the Kingdom of Madagascar, which by 1828 controlled much of the island.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antananarivo.jpg" alt="Antananarivo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Anosy,_Central_Antananarivo,_Capital_of_Madagascar,_Photo_by_Sascha_Grabow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sascha Grabow www.saschagrabow.com, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Merina economy rested on irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> grown in the valleys and marshes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a> organised communal labour to drain and dike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betsimitatatra">Betsimitatatra</a> plain, and rice became the basis of royal power, taxation and population growth. Society was divided into the <em>andriana</em>, ranked noble descent groups; the <em>hova</em>, free commoners organised in local descent groups and village assemblies, the <em>fokonolona</em>; and the <em>andevo</em>, enslaved people, whose numbers grew sharply through war and purchase. All free subjects owed the crown <em>fanompoana</em>, unpaid labour service, which the nineteenth-century state extended to building, mining, porterage and soldiering so heavily that many fled or resisted. Markets were regulated by royal authority, and payments were made in Spanish and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> dollars cut into small pieces and weighed on scales. In the late eighteenth century the sale of captives to French planters of Réunion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> financed Andrianampoinimerina's rise, as Pier Larson has shown; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a>'s treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> banned the export of slaves in 1817, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> continued until the French abolished it in 1896. Religion centred on royal talismans, the <em>sampy</em>, and on the ancestors, honoured in elaborate family tombs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestant Christianity</a>, spread by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a>, became the established religion of the court after 1869.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina.jpg" alt="Andrianampoinimerina" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrianampoinimerina_HQ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe-Auguste Ramanankirahina (1860-1915), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a> reunified Imerina with armies of free subjects raised through the village communities and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> bought from European traders in exchange for captives. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> (r. 1810–1828) transformed this force. Under the treaty of 1817 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> supplied arms, uniforms and instructors, and with the help of the British agent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-hastie">James Hastie</a> Radama built a standing army of several thousand regular soldiers drilled on European lines, supported by mass levies. With it he took the east-coast port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Toamasina</a> in 1817, campaigned against the coastal and southern peoples, and in 1824 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Mahajanga</a>, the chief port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakalava">Sakalava</a> kingdom of Boina. By his death Merina garrisons, many in fortified hilltop posts, held the central highlands and much of the east and north, though the Sakalava of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menabe">Menabe</a> and the peoples of the far south were never fully subdued. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a> continued conscription and campaigns, and in 1845 her forces repelled a joint British and French attack on Toamasina. Later reforms under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainilaiarivony">Rainilaiarivony</a> introduced general conscription and hired foreign officers, but the army remained poorly paid and supplied. It held off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the war of 1883–85 but collapsed before the expedition of 1895 under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-duchesne">Jacques Duchesne</a>, which marched from Mahajanga to the capital and shelled it into surrender on 30 September 1895.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/radama-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radama-i.jpg" alt="Radama I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radama_I,_portrait_by_Ramanankirahina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe-Auguste Ramanankirahina (1860-1915), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Merina kingship combined sacred authority with an increasingly bureaucratic state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianampoinimerina">Andrianampoinimerina</a> governed through public assemblies, the <em>kabary</em>, at which he proclaimed laws in elaborate oratory, through the fokonolona of each village, and through officials who supervised land, labour and justice; his laws punished theft, regulated markets and defined the obligations of each descent group. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> introduced a system of numbered military and civil ranks, the 'honours', and with the help of missionaries a written administration in Malagasy, using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet adopted in the 1820s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a> (r. 1828–1861) relied on powerful hova ministers and officers, used the <em>tangena</em> poison ordeal against suspected enemies, expelled the missionaries and from 1835 banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, executing a number of converts. After the brief reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-ii">Radama II</a>, killed in 1863, real power passed to the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainilaiarivony">Rainilaiarivony</a>, who held office from 1864 to 1895 and married three successive queens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasoherina">Rasoherina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-ii">Ranavalona II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii">Ranavalona III</a>. He built a modern state on paper and partly in practice: ministries on European lines and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-305-articles">Code of 305 Articles</a> in 1881, provincial governors, registers of land and population, and compulsory schooling. Ranavalona II's baptism in 1869 and the public burning of the royal sampy made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> an instrument of government, with church and school networks reaching across the highlands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rova-of-antananarivo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rova-of-antananarivo.jpg" alt="Rova of Antananarivo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rova-of-antananarivo">Rova of Antananarivo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstructed_Rova_Antananarivo_Madagascar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hery Zo Rakotondramanana, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Merina kingdom created the first state that claimed, and largely achieved, authority over most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, and its capital, language and institutions became the core of the modern nation. Standard written Malagasy developed from the Merina dialect, which missionaries and the court fixed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet; the complete Bible was published in Malagasy in 1835, one of the first full Bible translations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>, and literacy spread more widely in nineteenth-century Imerina than in most of the continent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a> grew from a hilltop royal town into the island's capital, crowned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rova-of-antananarivo">Rova</a>, whose palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manjakamiadana">Manjakamiadana</a> was built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> by the Frenchman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-laborde">Jean Laborde</a> and later encased in stone by the missionary architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cameron-missionary">James Cameron</a>. The royal hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambohimanga">Ambohimanga</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. The kingdom also left a divided legacy. Its conquests, labour levies and the enslavement of captives from other regions created lasting resentment of Merina dominance among coastal peoples, a division that French colonial rule exploited through its 'politique des races' and that continued to shape Malagasy politics after independence. Its diplomacy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> made it one of the few African states of the nineteenth century treated as a sovereign partner in international treaties.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/franco-hova-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franco-hova-war.jpg" alt="Franco-Hova War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/franco-hova-war">Franco-Hova War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LaGuerreAMadagascar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis Bombled / Henri Galli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom's fall came from French imperial ambitions, which built on claims dating back to seventeenth-century trading posts and on French influence among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakalava">Sakalava</a> of the north-west. In 1883 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> attacked, bombarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Mahajanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Toamasina</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-hova-war">Franco-Hova War</a> ended with the treaty of December 1885, which gave France <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antsiranana">Diego Suarez</a>, a large indemnity and a claim to control <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>'s foreign relations, though the Merina government disputed the meaning of the terms. In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Madagascar in exchange for French recognition of British control of Zanzibar, leaving the kingdom without a protector. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainilaiarivony">Rainilaiarivony</a> rejected French demands, France sent an expedition of some 15,000 men under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-duchesne">Jacques Duchesne</a> in 1895. Several thousand French soldiers died, overwhelmingly from disease, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a> fell on 30 September and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii">Ranavalona III</a> signed a protectorate treaty. Rainilaiarivony was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, where he died in 1896. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menalamba-rebellion">Menalamba</a> ('red shawl') rising against the French and the Christian Merina elite spread through the highlands, and in August 1896 France annexed the island as a colony. Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni">Joseph Gallieni</a> suppressed the revolt, executed leading Merina officials and on 28 February 1897 abolished the monarchy, sending Ranavalona III into exile on Réunion and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, where she died in 1917.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Merina Kingdom turned a cluster of highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing chiefdoms into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, conquering most of the island with British-trained armies, adopting writing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestant Christianity</a> and modern administration, and leaving the capital, language and institutions on which colonial and independent Madagascar were built after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> abolished it in 1897.</em></p>

